---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Figure from "../components/Figure.astro";
import PresentationFigure from "../components/PresentationFigure.astro";
import ResearchClip from "../components/ResearchClip.astro";
import Citation from "../components/Citation.astro";
import MediaCredit from "../components/MediaCredit.astro";
---
<Layout>
  <Hero />
  <div class="container visual-overview">
    <section class="section abstract-section" id="abstract" aria-labelledby="abstract-title">
      <h2 id="abstract-title">A local decision. A shared road.</h2>
      <p class="section-deck">At an intersection, a robot vehicle makes a small decision: Stop or Go. Repeated across a road network, those decisions interact with human drivers and traffic signals. We study how a shared reinforcement-learning policy can turn local choices into better mixed-traffic flow in simulation.</p>
      <ResearchClip id="network" />
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="method" aria-labelledby="method-title">
      <h2 id="method-title">Learn at the intersection.</h2>
      <p class="section-deck">Observe local traffic. Choose Stop or Go. Coordinate through shared roads.</p>
      <div class="method-media">
        <PresentationFigure id="method" concise />
        <div class="method-context">
          <p>A shared Rainbow DQN policy controls robot vehicles near intersections. Human-driven vehicles follow the Intelligent Driver Model; robot vehicles use the same driver model outside the control zone.</p>
          <ResearchClip id="junction" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="intersection-grid"><PresentationFigure id="signalized" concise /><PresentationFigure id="unsignalized" concise /></div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
      <h2 id="results-title">Look beyond a single junction.</h2>
      <p class="section-deck">Selected combinations of robot vehicles and signal control improve network flow in simulation. The benefit depends on the configuration and robot-vehicle share; adding unsignalized intersections does not always help, and some junctions still benefit from signals.</p>
      <p class="scope-note">The clips illustrate the simulation setting. They do not identify the controller or demonstrate the reported improvements; Waymo footage provides independent real-world context.</p>
      <div class="results-media"><ResearchClip id="flow" /><Figure id="network" concise /></div>
    </section>
    <section class="section vision-section" id="vision" aria-labelledby="vision-title"><h2 id="vision-title">From shared roads to a shared transport system.</h2><p class="section-intro">A local policy is one piece of a city-scale transport system. Moving beyond these simulations requires traffic engineers, robot-learning researchers, public agencies, and human-behavior specialists to connect vehicle decisions with signal operations and real road conditions. The vision is coordinated mobility as automated and human-driven vehicles continue to share the network.</p></section>
    <Citation />
    <details class="reading-details media-details" id="media-descriptions"><summary>Video descriptions & sources</summary><MediaCredit /></details>
  </div>
</Layout>
